.TH "LAB" "1" "September 26, 2026" "Labrys" "User Commands"
.SH Name
lab \- manage Kubeflow notebooks from a terminal
.SH Synopsis
.IP
.EX
lab [\-\-cluster CLUSTER]
lab init
lab cluster add|remove [\-\-cluster\-id CLUSTER]
lab notebook COMMAND \-\-cluster CLUSTER [OPTIONS]
lab auth \-\-request PATH
lab \-\-help
lab \-\-version
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]labrys\f[R] is an alias for \f[CR]lab\f[R].
Both commands accept the same arguments.
.SH Description
Labrys connects to a Kubernetes cluster using credentials stored in
1Password.
It can create a Kubeflow Notebook, open a container shell or VS Code
window, and start, stop or delete the Notebook.
.PP
Each invocation owns one authorized connection to one cluster.
Keep lab running while using its shell or editor connection.
Exiting lab disconnects local clients but leaves remote Notebooks
running.
Use \f[CR]stop\f[R] when you want to release a Notebook\(cqs compute
resources.
.PP
Run \f[CR]lab\f[R] without a subcommand to use the interactive menu.
Run \f[CR]lab notebook COMMAND\f[R] to perform one operation with
explicit arguments.
.SH Setup
Install Labrys and its manual as described in the source checkout\(cqs
README.
Setup needs the 1Password desktop app and a read\-only Service Account
with access to the vault holding lab\(cqs index, cluster credentials and
local\-data encryption key.
.PP
Run \f[CR]lab init\f[R], enter the Service Account Token in the hidden
prompt, and follow the instructions to create or select the encryption
key and connection index.
Choose an absolute directory for private cluster profiles.
Because the Service Account is read\-only, you save the proposed
documents in the 1Password app; lab reads them back to verify the
content.
Copy copies the full document but does not save it in 1Password.
.PP
Prepare \f[CR]CLUSTER.yaml\f[R] in that directory using your
cluster\(cqs creation rules.
The checkout contains \f[CR]profiles/template.yaml\f[R] and the
repository\-local \f[CR]lab\-profile\f[R] Agent Skill.
The template uses fictional values and must be adapted to your
environment.
Register the prepared profile, then open a session:
.IP
.EX
lab cluster add \-\-cluster\-id research\-example
lab \-\-cluster research\-example
.EE
.PP
Shell and editor access require \f[CR]kubectl\f[R] on \f[CR]PATH\f[R].
SSH connections also require \f[CR]ssh\f[R] and an existing SSH host
configuration.
Editor access requires \f[CR]code\f[R] on \f[CR]PATH\f[R]; the adapter
currently accepts VS Code 1.137.0 with Dev Containers 0.469.0.
Other versions are rejected by the compatibility check.
.SH Interactive use
The session menu offers Notebook actions and connection controls.
Choose an action or type a Notebook command without the
\f[CR]lab notebook\f[R] prefix, for example
\f[CR]list \-\-namespace research\f[R].
Forms ask for missing values and show the manifest before creation.
.PP
Within the menu, \f[CR]status\f[R] describes the lab connection;
\f[CR]notebook status\f[R] describes a Notebook.
\f[CR]disconnect\f[R] or \f[CR]exit\f[R] closes the session.
After a connection loss, \f[CR]reconnect\f[R] restores access using the
original authorization deadline.
Use another terminal for a second session or cluster.
.PP
Tab and Shift\-Tab move between controls, Enter advances or submits, and
Escape returns.
Tab accepts a grey field suggestion; typing replaces it.
Up and Down select history or suggestions.
F1 or Help explains the focused field.
.PP
After a new Notebook becomes Ready, you can save its settings as a
preset.
Presets exclude the instance name and are scoped to the cluster and
namespace.
Loading a preset fills the form; editing the form does not change the
saved preset.
The menu\(cqs \f[CR]save\-preset\f[R] action can also save a completed
creation by operation ID.
.SH AI agents and automation
Use standalone \f[CR]lab notebook\f[R] commands with explicit arguments
rather than driving the interactive menu.
Add \f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] for machine\-readable results and
\f[CR]\-\-request\-auth\f[R] when a person will supply the Service
Account Token.
For example, an agent can request a Notebook listing without asking the
user to disclose a token in chat:
.IP
.EX
lab notebook list \-\-cluster research\-example \-\-namespace research \(rs
  \-\-json \-\-request\-auth
.EE
.IP "1." 3
Start the command and keep that process running.
It prints an authorization command to stderr and waits up to five
minutes.
The authorization command uses the installed Python interpreter to
invoke \f[CR]lab auth \-\-request PATH\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
Show the printed command to the user unchanged.
The user runs it in their own terminal, reviews the requested operation
and enters the token in a hidden prompt.
The token goes directly to the waiting lab process through a local
socket; it is not part of the printed command or the request file.
.IP "3." 3
Wait for the original process to finish, then read its exit code and
JSON output.
Successful token delivery means only that lab received the token; the
original process reports whether authorization and the operation
succeeded.
.PP
Keep the process alive through the user\(cqs response; a short
command\-runner timeout can cancel the request.
Poll the existing process instead of launching another copy.
Each standalone invocation needs its own authorization.
An expired request requires a new invocation and its newly printed
authorization command.
.PP
For start, stop, delete or retry, use \f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] only for the
operation the user has authorized.
Token handoff does not bypass the operation\(cqs confirmation prompt.
Standalone \f[CR]create\f[R] submits after authorization without a
second confirmation.
After an interrupted creation, retain its \f[CR]operation_id\f[R] and
inspect that operation before retrying; a lost response does not
establish that creation failed.
.PP
\f[CR]init\f[R] and \f[CR]cluster\f[R] require interactive setup.
\f[CR]shell\f[R] and \f[CR]open\f[R] need a controlling terminal and do
not support JSON output.
\f[CR]\-\-token\-stdin\f[R] is an alternative for a trusted process that
already supplies the token through a pipe; do not put tokens in command
arguments, environment variables, chat or tool output.
.PP
\f[CR]lab\-credential\f[R] is the internal Kubernetes credential helper
invoked by kubectl and editor clients.
It uses an existing session grant to return a Kubernetes credential to
that client.
It does not prompt for a 1Password token or authorize an agent\(cqs
command.
Agents request authorization with \f[CR]\-\-request\-auth\f[R]; the user
provides it through \f[CR]lab auth\f[R].
.SH Commands
.SS init
Configure this device as described under Setup.
When selecting an existing index, lab checks its encryption key against
any local encrypted data.
.SS cluster add
Register a prepared \f[CR].yaml\f[R] profile.
Omit \f[CR]\-\-cluster\-id\f[R] to select an unregistered profile from
the configured directory.
Unavailable entries explain their error; Refresh rereads the directory
and index.
.PP
Choose an existing 1Password connection note, paste a kubeconfig, or
import a kubeconfig file.
For imports, select the context and route, review the connection and
save the proposed note in 1Password.
lab verifies the connection, then asks you to save and verify the
updated index.
Registration does not create a Notebook.
.SS cluster remove
Remove a registration from the index.
Omit \f[CR]\-\-cluster\-id\f[R] to select an entry.
Save the proposed index in 1Password and confirm its read\-back.
Removing a registration leaves the profile, connection note, local
history and remote Notebooks intact.
Existing lab sessions remain authorized.
.PP
Avoid concurrent index edits: a manual save and lab\(cqs read\-back are
separate operations.
Recheck repeats verification; Cancel does not undo a saved change.
.SS notebook list
List Notebooks and their observed state in one namespace.
.SS notebook create
Create a Notebook from explicit fields or a saved preset.
The standalone command prints a manifest preview and submits it without
a confirmation prompt.
The interactive form asks for confirmation.
.PP
The default result is \f[CR]Accepted\f[R], meaning Kubernetes accepted
the object.
Use \f[CR]\-\-wait\f[R] to wait for a Ready Pod.
A failed or expired wait leaves the Notebook on the cluster; inspect its
status before retrying.
.SS notebook status NAME
Show a Notebook\(cqs configured resources, requested lifecycle and
observed Pods.
Use \f[CR]\-\-operation\-id\f[R] instead of name and namespace to
inspect a recorded creation.
Resource values describe requests, not measured usage.
.PP
\f[CR]Running\f[R] means an owned Pod is Ready.
A stop request remains \f[CR]Stopping\f[R] until the owned Pods are
gone, then becomes \f[CR]Stopped\f[R].
Other states include \f[CR]Starting\f[R], \f[CR]Error\f[R] and
\f[CR]Unknown\f[R].
A failed API query is reported as an error.
.SS notebook shell NAME
Open a shell in a Ready container.
\f[CR]\-\-pod\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-container\f[R] resolve ambiguous
targets.
A controlling terminal is required.
The remote shell is owned by this lab invocation; start a terminal
multiplexer yourself if you need one.
.SS notebook open NAME
Request a VS Code window attached to a Ready container.
The lab process stays in the foreground to keep the editor connection
authorized.
Exiting lab leaves the window open but disconnected.
A new authorized session opens a new window.
.SS notebook start|stop|delete NAME
Start or stop the Notebook through the Kubeflow controller, or delete
the Notebook object.
These commands confirm the selected object before changing it.
\f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] skips that confirmation for this invocation.
.PP
Start and stop return when the change is accepted, before the controller
finishes.
Use \f[CR]status\f[R] to observe progress.
Stopping releases the running Pod while retaining the Notebook
configuration.
Deleting also removes that configuration.
lab does not delete the files in the host directory mounted as Notebook
data; temporary container and \f[CR]emptyDir\f[R] data do not survive
Pod removal.
.SS notebook retry
Reconcile a recorded creation using \f[CR]\-\-operation\-id\f[R].
If the original object exists, verify its identity.
Submit again only after a confirmed absence and a check against the
current profile.
A changed profile or conflicting object blocks replay.
The command asks for confirmation unless \f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] is supplied.
.SS notebook editor\-restart NAME
This operation is currently unavailable: no image/build profiles are
qualified, so it returns \f[CR]Unsupported\f[R] before remote execution.
\f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] cannot bypass this check.
Ordinary \f[CR]open\f[R] is available with the editor versions listed
under Setup.
.SS auth \(enrequest PATH
Supply a token to one waiting \f[CR]\-\-request\-auth\f[R] invocation
from another terminal.
Run the exact command printed by the requester.
Review the displayed operation, then enter the token in the hidden
prompt.
This command accepts only \f[CR]\-\-request\f[R].
It does not create a reusable authorization service.
.SH Options
Common Notebook options can appear before or after \f[CR]notebook\f[R]
or its command.
Standalone Notebook commands require \f[CR]\-\-cluster\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-namespace\f[R].
Commands targeting an existing Notebook also require its positional
name.
An operation ID supplies name and namespace for \f[CR]status\f[R] and
\f[CR]retry\f[R]; omit both in that case.
Inside a session, forms can ask for missing values.
.PP
